==English==
{{wikipedia}}
{{wikipedia|Chorion (egg)}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|chorion}}, from {{cog|grc|χοριον||membrane surrounding the fetus, afterbirth}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|choria}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy|biology}} The [[protective]] and [[nutritive]] [[membrane]] in higher [[vertebrate]]s that [[attaches]] the [[fetus]] to the [[uterus]].
#* '''2002''', Rovert E. A. Stewart, Barbara E. Stewart, ''Female Reproductive Systems'', entry in ''Encyclopedia of Marine Mammals'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=RsEKkDNF5f4C&pg=PA425&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 425],
#*: The allantois fuses with the '''chorion''', forming a small round area, the allentochorion. This region becomes the placenta (Fig. 3).
#* '''2006''', Ona Marie Faye-Petersen, Debra S. Heller, Vijay V. Joshi, ''Handbook Of Placental Pathology'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=_gSq0Dw0wPYC&pg=PA4&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 4],
#*: In this illustration, some license is taken, since the amnion is shown as still separate from the '''chorion'''; this is done to illustrate the process of apposition of the amniotic sac to the '''chorion''' and to accentuate the interposition of the yolk sac between the amnion and the '''chorion'''.
#* '''2007''', Susan Tucker Blackburn, ''Maternal, Fetal, & Neonatal Physiology: A Clinical Perspective'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=2y6zOSQcn14C&pg=PA106&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 106],
#*: The amnion and '''chorion''' begin to develop soon after fertilization and continue to grow until about 28 weeks′ gestation.
# {{lb|en|biology|entomology}} The [[outer]] [[case]] of an [[insect]] [[egg]].
#* '''1994''', Klaus Urich, ''Comparative Animal Biochemistry'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=GLbcWyeaCGQC&pg=PA392&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 392],
#*: The '''chorion''' has a predominantly lamellar structure, the lamellae being two-dimensional networks of protein fibrils.
#* '''1998''', R. F. Chapman, ''The Insects: Structure and Function'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=jHUCdbgW4MAC&pg=PA326&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 326],
#*: Even closely related species may have '''chorions''' differing in thickness. In the silkmoths, for example, the '''chorion''' of ''Bombyx'' is about 25 μm thick while that of ''Hyalophora'' is 55 μm thick.
#* '''2008''', John L. Capinera, ''Eggs of Insects'', entry in John L. Capinera (editor), ''Encyclopedia of Entomology'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=i9ITMiiohVQC&pg=PA1290&dq=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=yJ2kUMbFJsvjmAXI4ICYDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chorion%22%7C%22chorions%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 1290],
#*: In addition to facilitating gas exchange and water conservation, the '''chorion''' must, in some cases, allow uptake of water or liquid nutrients from the environment.
# {{lb|en|botany}} The outer membrane of seeds of plants.

